[#4471](/kb/incidents/4471) is marked known: one issue, one fix queued. Its own table says otherwise.
The reports in it describe three problems. The queued parser fix closes only the first; the other two
keep coming in after it ships.

<Callout kind="warn">
Not one issue. Each problem below has its own ticket, with the reports it covers:
<a href={issues}>the tickets</a>.
</Callout>

## What #4471 holds

<CauseTable ticket={4471} />

## The three problems

- **The parser** (the `parser` row). The shared
  parser reads dates month first, and every tenant on file writes the day first. Day and month come
  out swapped, on every channel.
- **The booking-v3 date hint** (the `hint` row). booking-v3 prints `MM/DD/YYYY` under its date field.
  Day-first tenants on booking-v3 type the month first, as told, and the form reads it day first.
  Only through the form, only on booking-v3. A parser fix does not reach it. The table already says
  it: not this ticket, no ticket of its own.
- **One-day moves** (the note under the table). The booking moves by one day, or arrives a day early.
  A month-first parser swaps day and month; it never moves a date by one day. The desk read these
  right (`desk-notes.json`), then filed them under `parser` by tenant. Look at time zones and DST.

## How the tickets count

- One report per id. The ticket export pages overlap, so some reports come twice.
- The cause comes from the tenant (its locale and its booking form, `locales.ts`), not from the
  intake tag. Intake guesses from the reporter's browser.
- Each ticket counts what the table above shows. A one-day move is filed under `parser` by tenant,
  so it counts in the parser ticket, as in the table, and in the one-day ticket.

## Why it happened

[Decision 0001](/kb/decisions/0001-dedupe-by-subject) merges on subject alone, and nobody reads the
body. Reports with many different subjects ended up in one ticket because all of them say "wrong day".
The subject tells you a date is wrong; the tenant, the channel and the body tell you why.

